WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(bright music)

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.510
- [Narrator] This sculpture
garden located in Japan

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.520
was primarily unknown
to the world until 2016

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.011
when a photographer stumbled upon it.

00:00:16.011 --> 00:00:18.594
(bright music)

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.200
There are around 800
sculptures in this park,

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.870
depicting a mix of Buddhist
deities and what are believed

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.410
to be replicas of real people.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.970
All were commissioned by
a wealthy Japanese man

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.053
named Mitsurou Furukawa.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.370
He is said to have paid six billion yen,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.790
around 60 million U.S. dollars

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:44.140
to create this collection in 1989.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.310
There is a large statue of Furukawa,

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.499
which stands overlooking the park.

00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:51.082
(bright music)

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.000
The people depicted are said
to be his friends and family,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.540
but it is unclear if they had

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.853
any knowledge of their stony replicas.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.160
They stand near a Japanese village,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.580
the name of which
translates to the village

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.743
where you can meet Buddhist statues.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.430
Although these lifelike statues stare

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.680
with blank expressions, visitors say

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.430
there is an unsettling feeling
to this place and a concern

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.698
that at night these
aphogees aren't so still.

00:01:18.698 --> 00:01:21.281
(bright music)

